{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B051",
    "B053"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B046 without change. Magic-wavelength confinement solved a central experimental problem: many atoms can be interrogated while preserving the narrow clock transition. The resulting optical lattice clocks have become reproducible, high-performance instruments across laboratories, with demonstrated value for metrology, sensing, and tests of fundamental physics. Katori and Ye remain a particularly coherent pair for the decisive concept and its defining experimental realization. The case rests on a completed, measurable advance, not on a prospective standards decision or a hoped-for application.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary must remain narrow. Optical lattice clocks belong to a broader optical-frequency-metrology lineage, and archival review must test whether Katori and Ye alone appropriately represent the invention and defining realization. The citation should make no claim to credit all optical clocks, all subsequent implementations, or a future redefinition of the second.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that quantum error correction established the physical possibility of scalable computation, although a fully fault-tolerant scalable logical computer has not yet been realized.",
        "response": "I accept the foundational force of B027 and its importance to every serious quantum-computing programme. But the missing sustained fault-tolerant realization remains material when choosing the achievement for recognition now, especially since the proposed three-person boundary is broad and selective. B046 has already passed the corresponding experimental test: its defining principle produces reproducible measurement performance in independent laboratories."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson presents the quantum cascade laser as a mature new semiconductor-laser principle with decades of scientific and practical consequences, subject to clarification of credit for epitaxy, concept, and first realization.",
        "response": "I agree that B053 is a serious alternative and retain it as such. Its durable devices and new spectral reach are impressive. However, the unresolved question of whether enabling epitaxy, conceptual invention, and first realization can be represented by the proposed trio is central. B046 presently offers the sharper connection between a distinct physical idea, its realization, and its two credited names."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson favors WMAP because it supplied a durable, repeatedly checked empirical framework for cosmology, while recognizing the difficulty of restricted attribution within a mission-scale collaboration.",
        "response": "WMAP is an outstanding empirical achievement, but the mission, instrument, analysis, and interpretation roles make the proposed restricted attribution difficult to assess. Lattice clocks likewise have broad communities, yet the supplied record identifies a more delimited conceptual and experimental pair. My preference is based on that credit boundary and on direct experimental reproducibility, not on relative levels of support."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone are the appropriate laureates for the lattice-clock invention and defining realization.",
      "How precisely the citation can separate optical lattice clocks from the wider development of optical-frequency clocks."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile; my professional background in atomic physics and quantum optics creates general field familiarity but no disclosed direct connection to the proposed laureates."
  }
}
